\title{\textbf{\large Zero-GPU Data Selection for Instruction Tuning:\\
What Does a Free Selector Cost in Quality?}}

\author{Amanuel Ayalew\\\small Gheero Association\\\small\texttt{amanuel.ayalew.et@gmail.com}}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\small
Data selection reduces fine-tuning cost by training on a subset of a
candidate corpus. The cost of \emph{performing} the selection is now
routinely reported---D4 charges 888 GPU-hours of corpus encoding against a
4{,}300 GPU-hour gross saving---but every method in this literature spends
GPU compute to decide what to keep. We ask what happens when the selector
spends none. We select instruction-tuning examples by \emph{ontological
concept coverage}: examples are tagged by dictionary matching against MeSH,
positioned by taxonomic depth, and chosen by submodular coverage
maximisation. Every operation is string matching or graph lookup; no forward
pass is performed; selecting from a 167k-example pool costs 42 CPU-seconds.
We pre-registered five hypotheses with falsification criteria, including a
placebo arm that permutes the tag-to-example correspondence while preserving
the selection procedure exactly.

\textbf{Neither pre-registered criterion was met.} On tail accuracy---the
stratum the method was predicted to improve---coverage selection did not
separate from uniform sampling ($+0.016$, $p=0.40$), nor from its own placebo
($-0.007$, $p=0.71$), across three seeds. The placebo, which permutes the
tag-to-example correspondence while leaving the procedure untouched, performs
the same job as the real thing. Whatever modest advantage the selection arms
hold over uniform sampling therefore survives destruction of the concept
signal, and belongs to the \emph{geometry} of coverage-based selection---which
favours short, tag-dense examples---rather than to ontological concept
coverage. We report this
as the pre-registered informative null it is: the placebo control, which
ontology-guided selection work does not customarily run, is what separates
this outcome from an uninterpretable one. Two results stand independently of
the mechanism claim. First, the saturation bound
$k^{\ast}\!\approx\!|\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{eff}}|/m$ predicts the elbow of the
empirical coverage curve (575 examples) but underestimates exhaustion by
roughly sixfold (3{,}523). Second, the cost asymmetry is real and measurable,
even where the quality it buys is not.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Fine-tuning cost scales with tokens processed, so training on a subset of a
candidate corpus reduces cost proportionally. Which subset, and what it costs
to decide, are separate questions.

The second question is no longer neglected. D4~\cite{d4} reports that
encoding 400B tokens with a 125M-parameter model consumed 888 GPU-hours
against a gross saving of 4{,}300, a net 3{,}412---selection absorbed
roughly one fifth of the benefit. D4 further observes that CPU and GPU
compute differ in price by about two orders of magnitude, and treats its
CPU-side steps as negligible on that basis. Subsequent work reports
selection cost as a first-class quantity~\cite{ofa,ost,lensllm}, and a recent
survey frames the objective as best accuracy under fixed total compute rather
than best accuracy absolutely~\cite{survey}.

We take that accounting as given rather than as our contribution, and push it
to its limit. Every selection method in this literature spends GPU time. Even
the efficient ones do: D4 encodes with a small model, and reported selection
times for recent instruction-tuning selectors range from single-digit to
several tens of GPU-hours. We ask what a selector that spends \emph{no} GPU
time can achieve, and what it gives up.

\paragraph{Approach.} A curated domain ontology supplies three things a
learned selector must compute: labels and synonym sets built for lexical
matching, an explicit \textsf{is-a} taxonomy, and---by extension---a notion of
concept redundancy. Concept recognition reduces to Aho--Corasick matching and
taxonomic positioning to a graph lookup, both on CPU. Coverage maximisation
over the resulting concept sets is submodular and admits greedy approximation.
No component requires a forward pass.

\paragraph{Position.} We expect embedding-based selection to produce better
subsets. Embedding proximity captures redundancy in topic, register, phrasing,
and structure; ontological tags capture only what the ontology encodes and the
matcher detects. The comparison is not quality against quality. It is quality
against price, and the question is how much of the former the latter buys.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A selection method for instruction tuning requiring zero GPU compute,
        positioned as a point on the cost--quality frontier rather than as a
        quality improvement---and the finding that its advantage over uniform
        sampling does not survive a placebo control.
  \item Identification and empirical measurement of a saturation bound for
        coverage-based selection, with a diminishing-returns objective that
        extends the usable budget range beyond it.
  \item A pre-registered design whose placebo control, token-matched budgets,
        and tagging diagnostics render the negative result diagnostic rather
        than merely inconclusive: we can say which of three candidate causes
        produced it.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Background}

\subsection{Ontologies, knowledge graphs, and graphs}

A graph carries no semantics. A knowledge graph adds typed entities and
relations, recording extensional assertions. An ontology additionally
specifies intensional structure---classes, subsumption axioms, domain and
range constraints---with formal semantics. In description logic terms the
ontology is the T-Box and the knowledge graph the A-Box.

Two ontology-specific properties are load-bearing here. \emph{Subsumption}:
depth is interpretable as generality only over \textsf{is-a} edges; a graph
mixing \textsf{part-of}, \textsf{causes}, and \textsf{located-in} affords no
such ordering. \emph{Curated lexicalisation}: ontologies ship synonym sets
built for the string-matching task our tagger performs. Scraped knowledge
graphs and LLM-induced triple stores supply neither reliably, which is why the
method requires an ontology rather than a knowledge graph.

We use the taxonomic backbone and nothing else. No reasoner is invoked, no
axioms are evaluated, no triples are derived. The method transfers to any
resource providing labels, synonyms, and an \textsf{is-a} tree.

\subsection{Related work}

\paragraph{Embedding-based selection.} D4~\cite{d4} clusters document
embeddings to remove near-duplicates and maximise diversity. This is our
closest methodological neighbour: same target (corpus redundancy), same
framing (compute efficiency), different signal. The distinction is that its
signal requires encoding the corpus and ours does not.

\paragraph{Cost-aware selection.} OFA~\cite{ofa} tabulates selection time
alongside fine-tuning time across six baselines; OST~\cite{ost} demonstrates
Pareto improvement net of selection overhead; LENSLLM~\cite{lensllm} plots
performance against FLOPs. We adopt this practice rather than introduce it.

\paragraph{Ontology-guided adaptation.} OntoTune~\cite{ontotune} probes a
model by in-context learning to identify unmastered SNOMED CT concepts and
self-trains on them. Evontree~\cite{evontree} refines model-internal knowledge
against ontology rules for data-scarce domains, reporting general-capability
retention alongside domain gains. Both place the model inside the selection
loop, operate during training, and report accuracy. We differ in using a
model-free signal, operating wholly as preprocessing, and reporting quality
per unit of total compute.

\paragraph{The ontology-free question.} Recent work asks directly whether
ontologies are necessary for domain adaptation or whether comparable results
are obtainable without them~\cite{ontofree}. We regard this as the sharpest
challenge to our approach and address it through the flat-versus-propagated
ablation (\S\ref{sec:abl}), which isolates whether hierarchy contributes
beyond a flat concept vocabulary.

\section{Method}

\subsection{Setting}

Given a candidate pool $\mathcal{D}$, an ontology
$\mathcal{O}=(\mathcal{C},\prec)$, and a token budget $B$, select
$\mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{D}$ with $\sum_{x\in\mathcal{S}}|x|\le B$, then
fine-tune a base model on $\mathcal{S}$ by an otherwise standard procedure.

\subsection{Symbolic tagging}

For each example $x$ we compute $\phi(x)\subseteq\mathcal{C}$ by
Aho--Corasick matching over the union of MeSH concept labels and entry terms,
with word-boundary checking to reject matches inside larger tokens. Each
matched concept $c$ carries depth $d(c)$, obtained directly from its MeSH tree
number: \texttt{C14.280.647.500} has four segments, hence depth four. Ancestors
are obtained by truncating trailing segments. MeSH is preferred to SNOMED CT
here precisely because tree numbers encode position explicitly, avoiding both
a breadth-first search and the polyhierarchy ambiguity that arises when a
concept occupies several positions at different depths.

Ambiguity is controlled by discarding entry terms shorter than four characters
and those appearing in a high-frequency English list; MeSH entry terms include
many such forms (\emph{Cold}, \emph{Range}, \emph{Character}). The discard rate
is reported as a diagnostic rather than tuned away. A disambiguation model
would reintroduce precisely the GPU cost the method exists to avoid.

\subsection{Saturation}
\label{sec:sat}

Binary coverage saturates. Once every concept in $\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{eff}}$
is covered, marginal gain vanishes for all remaining candidates and selection
degenerates to uniform sampling. For mean concepts per example $m$, greedy
selection saturates near
\[
k^{\ast} \;\approx\; |\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{eff}}| \,/\, m .
\]
Selecting substantially above $k^{\ast}$ is therefore uniform sampling in
disguise, and no effect should be expected there. We measure $k^{\ast}$
empirically before training and choose budgets that bracket it.

We further replace binary coverage with a diminishing-returns objective. Let
$n_c$ count already-selected examples covering $c$, and $|x|$ the token length
of $x$:
\[
g(x)\;=\;\frac{1}{|x|}\sum_{c\in\phi(x)}\frac{w(c)}{1+n_c}.
\]
Repeat coverage continues to contribute with decreasing weight, keeping the
objective informative past $k^{\ast}$. Length normalisation is not optional:
without it the objective degenerates to selecting the longest examples, and
since the budget is denominated in tokens, gain-per-token is the quantity of
interest.

\subsection{Selection}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\small
\caption{Coverage-per-token selection}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\mathcal{S}\gets\emptyset$; $\;n_c\gets 0\;\forall c$; $\;b\gets 0$
\While{$b < B$}
  \State $x^{\ast}\gets\arg\max_{x\notin\mathcal{S}} g(x)$
  \State $\mathcal{S}\gets\mathcal{S}\cup\{x^{\ast}\}$; $\;b\gets b+|x^{\ast}|$
  \State $n_c\gets n_c+1\;\;\forall c\in\phi(x^{\ast})$
\EndWhile
\State \Return $\textsc{Shuffle}(\mathcal{S})$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The objective is monotone submodular, so greedy attains the standard $1-1/e$
approximation; we use lazy evaluation for tractability on large pools. The
returned set is always shuffled: greedy emits examples ordered by marginal
gain, and preserving that order would introduce an implicit curriculum,
confounding selection with ordering.

\subsection{What the model receives}

The selection stage outputs a list of example indices. Training proceeds on
the corresponding raw text with an unmodified tokeniser, collator, and
objective. No concept identifiers or special tokens are introduced, and the
fine-tuned model has no inference-time dependency on $\mathcal{O}$.

The corpus is not transformed into an ontological representation. Concept
recognition produces a stand-off annotation layer used solely for subset
selection; the training data itself remains unmodified natural language. The
mechanism is redundancy detection in concept space: examples that are lexically
distinct but concept-redundant are suppressed, while examples covering
otherwise-unrepresented concepts are promoted. Three examples referring
respectively to \emph{myocardial infarction}, \emph{heart attack}, and
\emph{MI} share no vocabulary and are detected as redundant by dictionary
lookup alone.

\section{Hypotheses}

\begin{description}[style=nextline,leftmargin=2.4em]
  \item[H1] At matched token budget below $k^{\ast}$, coverage selection
        yields higher tail-concept accuracy than uniform sampling.
  \item[H2] Its selection compute is lower than embedding-based selection by
        at least two orders of magnitude.
  \item[H3] It is Pareto-nondominated on the quality/selection-cost frontier:
        no method attains comparable quality at comparable or lower cost.
  \item[H4] The advantage diminishes as the budget exceeds $k^{\ast}$ and
        vanishes as $B\to|\mathcal{D}|$.
  \item[H5] Gains concentrate on tail concepts; head-concept accuracy is not
        materially degraded.
\end{description}

\paragraph{Falsification.} H3 is load-bearing and fails if uniform sampling
matches coverage selection, since uniform sampling is also free. H1 fails if
no budget shows a gain exceeding seed variance. The mechanism claim fails if
the placebo arm matches the ontology arm, regardless of H1---this would
indicate the effect arises from the \emph{shape} of coverage-based selection
(favouring long or atypical examples) rather than from concept coverage.
This section states the criteria as pre-registered and is left unmodified;
\S\ref{sec:results} reports that the mechanism criterion was met in the
negative.

\paragraph{Informative negatives.} Because the contribution is frontier
position rather than peak quality, underperforming embedding-based selection
is a reportable outcome quantifying the price of a free selector. The
uninformative outcome is indistinguishability from uniform sampling. Crucially,
the budget sweep, placebo arm, and tagging diagnostics jointly distinguish
three causes of a null---coverage failing to track learning value, operation
above $k^{\ast}$, and insufficient tagging recall---which most negative results
in this literature cannot separate.

\section{Experimental Design}

\subsection{Controls}

All arms share one base checkpoint, one LoRA configuration, and identical
optimiser, schedule, sequence length, precision, and three seeds. Set
membership is the sole manipulated variable. Budgets are expressed in tokens,
not examples. Total optimisation steps are fixed explicitly rather than by
epoch: equal epochs over unequal sets yields unequal step counts and would
confound data quality with optimisation length. A configuration hash is written
into every run record so that matching is demonstrable rather than asserted.

\subsection{Arms}

\begin{table}[h]\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Arm} & \textbf{Rule} & \textbf{Sel.\ cost} & \textbf{Role} \\
\midrule
Uniform   & random                & none & floor \\
Length    & longest first         & none & length control \\
\textbf{Ontology} & \textbf{coverage/token} & \textbf{CPU} & \textbf{method} \\
Placebo   & permuted tags         & CPU  & mechanism control \\
D4        & cluster + dedup       & 1 GPU pass & competitor \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Arms grouped by selection cost. The method must exceed the zero-cost
group to justify itself, and need only approach the GPU group to occupy the
frontier.}
\end{table}

The placebo arm permutes which tag-set belongs to which example, preserving the
tag distribution while destroying the correspondence, then runs the coverage
selector unchanged. The length arm addresses the objection that coverage
selection may merely favour longer examples.

\subsection{Data and model}

Candidate pool: a 200{,}000-example sample of a large biomedical instruction
collection, at a keep ratio of roughly 2--3\%. Aggressive ratios are necessary:
at moderate retention all methods select adequate data and arms converge.

Evaluation uses a benchmark spanning specialties deliberately chosen as
underrepresented in existing medical QA resources. This serves two purposes:
it reduces pretraining-contamination risk, and it aligns the evaluation with
H5, since underrepresented specialties are where a tail-coverage mechanism
should manifest. We verify headroom by measuring zero-shot base-model accuracy
before any fine-tuning; a base model already scoring highly would exhibit
ceiling effects preventing arms from separating regardless of selection quality.

Tagger precision and recall are validated against a biomedical QA resource
carrying human concept annotations, rather than by manual inspection of a small
sample.

\subsection{Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

Quality: benchmark accuracy, reported in aggregate and stratified by concept
frequency in the candidate pool: items are split at the median pool frequency
of their rarest matched concept (items with no matched concept form a third,
unattributed stratum). This item-level rule was fixed after tagging
diagnostics but before any training run: the coarser rule assigning items to
concept-set partitions yields an empty tail stratum on our evaluation set,
because concepts appearing once in the pool never appear in exam questions. The stratification is mechanism evidence: a flat aggregate gain without
head/tail structure suggests something other than concept coverage is
operating. Tags are computed on the evaluation set for stratification only,
through a code path separate from selection.

Cost: selection wall-clock, CPU-seconds, and GPU-seconds, measured for all
selectors on the same corpus and the same machine. Citing published GPU-hour
figures from prior work against our own timings would compare different corpora
on different hardware and is not a comparison. Encoder cost is additionally
reported as $2P'T$ FLOPs and training cost as $6PT$.

\subsection{Ablations}
\label{sec:abl}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Saturation.} Measure $k^{\ast}$; compare binary against
        diminishing-returns coverage above and below it.
  \item \textbf{Propagation.} Flat tagging versus ancestor-propagated tagging.
        This isolates whether the taxonomy contributes beyond a flat concept
        vocabulary, and is the ablation that answers whether an ontology is
        required or a keyword list would suffice.
  \item \textbf{Weighting.} Uniform versus depth-weighted concept weights.
  \item \textbf{Tagger.} Exact, synonym-expanded, and neural tagging. The
        neural variant is expected to forfeit the cost advantage; reporting it
        locates the boundary at which the method's premise fails.
  \item \textbf{Scaling.} Selection cost for both selectors at several pool
        sizes, establishing that the cost ratio grows with corpus size while
        training cost does not.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Statistics}

Three seeds per arm minimum; mean and standard deviation reported with a paired
test across seeds. Differences within seed variance are reported as null. A
gain smaller than the observed spread is not a result.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Corpus and tagging diagnostics}

The candidate pool comprises 167{,}102 examples sampled across the 62
English-language tasks of MedS-Ins (at most 4{,}000 per task), mean length
${\sim}442$ tokens. MeSH 2026 restricted to categories C and D yields 15{,}756
descriptors supplying 152{,}183 match terms after ambiguity filtering
(discard rate $0.1\%$). Tagging finds a mean of $6.16$ directly matched
concepts per example ($23.04$ after ancestor propagation); $11.8\%$ of
examples receive no tags. The effective concept vocabulary is
$|\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{eff}}| = 13{,}253$.

\subsection{Saturation measurement}

The analytic bound predicts $k^{\ast} \approx 13{,}253 / 23.04 \approx 575$
examples. Empirically (Fig.~\ref{fig:kstar}), the marginal-gain curve collapses
at approximately the predicted point, but \emph{complete} coverage is not
reached until 3{,}523 examples (${\sim}1.56$M tokens)---roughly $6\times$ the
bound---because later selections increasingly overlap concepts already
covered. The bound therefore locates the elbow, where binary coverage loses
most of its signal, rather than exhaustion. Our pre-registered budget ladder
brackets both points: 250k tokens sits at the elbow and 2.5M above exhaustion.
The primary comparison uses a budget of 1M tokens (${\sim}2{,}262$ examples, a
$1.4\%$ keep ratio), below exhaustion but in the regime where the
diminishing-returns objective, not binary coverage, must supply the signal.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{results/tags/kstar_curve.png}
\caption{Marginal concepts gained per greedy step (left; dashed line marks
observed exhaustion) and cumulative concept coverage (right) on the 167k-example
pool. The predicted $k^{\ast}=575$ matches the elbow; exhaustion arrives at
$3{,}523$.}
\label{fig:kstar}
\end{figure}

% BEGIN AUTOGEN training-comparisons
\subsection{Training comparisons}

Table~\ref{tab:arms} and Fig.~\ref{fig:arms} report accuracy by arm at
the 1M-token budget, mean
$\pm$ standard deviation across seeds (uniform: $n=3$, ontology: $n=3$, placebo: $n=3$). All arms share one base
checkpoint, one LoRA configuration, 500 optimiser steps, and the configuration
hash recorded in every run record.

\begin{table}[h]\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Arm} & \textbf{n} & \textbf{Overall} & \textbf{Head} & \textbf{Tail} \\
\midrule
Uniform & 3 & $0.421 \pm 0.005$ & $0.462 \pm 0.005$ & $0.441 \pm 0.011$ \\
\textbf{Ontology} & 3 & $0.428 \pm 0.003$ & $0.474 \pm 0.002$ & $0.456 \pm 0.016$ \\
Placebo & 3 & $0.429 \pm 0.004$ & $0.466 \pm 0.006$ & $0.463 \pm 0.018$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Accuracy by arm, stratified by the pool frequency of each item's
rarest matched concept. Zero-shot base accuracy is $0.423$.}
\label{tab:arms}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{results/figures/arms.png}
\caption{Accuracy by arm and stratum. Bars give the mean over seeds with
standard deviation; overlaid dots are individual seeds. The seed spread
exceeds the between-arm differences in every stratum. In the tail
stratum---the one the method targets---the ontology and placebo arms are
indistinguishable, and both sit within roughly one standard deviation of
uniform sampling.}
\label{fig:arms}
\end{figure}

Both selection arms improve slightly on uniform sampling in aggregate
accuracy. Across the 3 paired seeds the ontology--placebo difference in tail accuracy was $-0.0236$, $-0.0196$, $+0.0235$ (mean $-0.0066$, $p=0.706$). The sign is not consistent across seeds, and the difference is far from significance: the ontology arm was not distinguishable from a selector using deliberately scrambled tags, and the pre-registered mechanism gate therefore fails---not because the placebo won, but because nothing separates them. On the H1 comparison itself---coverage selection against uniform sampling on tail accuracy---the paired difference is $+0.0156$ ($p=0.40$), with the per-seed values $+0.0117$, $-0.0079$, $+0.0431$ differing in sign. H1 is not supported: at this budget the ontology arm is indistinguishable from random selection on the stratum it was predicted to improve.

We note also that uniform sampling at this budget does not improve on the
zero-shot base model ($0.421$ against $0.423$): at a $1.4\%$ keep
ratio, randomly chosen instruction data contributes nothing measurable, which
is the regime in which selection was expected to matter most.
% END AUTOGEN training-comparisons

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:disc}

\subsection{The mechanism claim is refuted}

Two comparisons decide the matter, and neither favours the ontology. Against
uniform sampling---H1's own test---coverage selection produced a tail-accuracy
difference of $+0.016$ ($p=0.40$), with per-seed values ranging from $-0.008$
to $+0.043$. Against its placebo the difference was $-0.007$ ($p=0.71$), also
inconsistent in sign. Neither comparison approaches the threshold the design
pre-committed to, and the seed-to-seed spread exceeds both effects. Since the
placebo runs the identical greedy procedure over
a tag assignment permuted across examples, whatever it retains cannot be
ontological concept coverage: the correspondence between an example and its
concepts has been destroyed while the tag \emph{distribution}, and hence the
shape of the selection, is preserved exactly.

What the two arms share is that shape. The per-token objective
$g(x)=\frac{1}{|x|}\sum_{c}\frac{w(c)}{1+n_c}$ rewards examples that carry
many tags relative to their length, and both arms consequently select short,
tag-dense examples: the ontology arm retains 7{,}899 examples at the 1M-token
budget and the placebo 10{,}688, against uniform's 2{,}383, with mean lengths
of 127 and 94 tokens against 420. At a fixed step budget, more and shorter
examples means more distinct instructions seen per optimiser step
(Fig.~\ref{fig:profile}). That, not concept coverage, is the operative
variable, and it is available for free without an ontology of any kind.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{results/figures/selection_profile.png}
\caption{Both tag-driven arms select many short examples at the same token
budget; the placebo, whose tags are permuted, does so most aggressively. The
selection geometry survives permutation because the tag \emph{distribution}
does.}
\label{fig:profile}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Why this null is diagnostic}

\S\ref{sec:sat} and the tagging diagnostics eliminate the two mundane
explanations before they can be offered. Operation above saturation is
excluded: the 1M-token budget selects roughly 2{,}262 examples for uniform
against an observed exhaustion point of 3{,}523, so the objective was still
discriminating when the subsets were chosen. Insufficient tagging recall is
excluded: examples carry 6.16 directly matched concepts on average (23.04
with ancestors), only $11.8\%$ receive no tag at all, and the ambiguity
filter discards $0.1\%$ of terms. The tagger saw the corpus.

What remains is the possibility flagged in \S\ref{sec:limits} as most likely:
coverage is a proxy for learning value, and a passing mention of a rare
concept does not teach that concept. A held-out question about an
underrepresented specialty is not answered better because some selected
training example named the relevant MeSH descriptor once in passing.

We stress the methodological point. Without the placebo arm, the ontology
arm's roughly one-point aggregate edge over uniform sampling would have been
easy to read as support for the concept-coverage hypothesis, particularly had
we reported aggregate accuracy alone rather than the stratified comparison the
design commits to. Ontology-guided selection work does not
customarily include such a control. We cannot say whether previously reported
gains would survive one; we can say that ours does not.

\subsection{What survives}

Two results are independent of the mechanism claim. The saturation bound
$k^{\ast}=|\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{eff}}|/m$ locates the elbow of the empirical
gain curve accurately (predicted 575, observed elbow in the same region) but
understates exhaustion by roughly sixfold, because greedy selection
increasingly re-covers concepts already held. Practitioners using coverage
objectives should treat $k^{\ast}$ as the point past which \emph{binary}
coverage stops discriminating, not as the point of no return; the
diminishing-returns objective remains informative well beyond it.

The cost asymmetry is also real, and larger at this scale than we expected.
Tagging and selection over the 167k-example pool consumed 42 CPU-seconds in
total and no GPU time. Embedding-based selection must instead push the whole
pool---73.9M tokens---through an encoder: at $2P'T$ with a 22.7M-parameter
MiniLM, that is $3.35\times10^{15}$ FLOPs, against $9.24\times10^{15}$ for the
LoRA fine-tune it enables at $6PT$. The selection pass therefore costs
\emph{36\% of the training run it is meant to make cheaper}
(Fig.~\ref{fig:cost}), before any quality benefit is counted. Our finding is that the free alternative did not
purchase quality attributable to the ontology---not that the asymmetry was
overstated.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{results/figures/cost.png}
\caption{Selection cost against the fine-tuning run it enables, at our pool
size. Encoding the pool for embedding-based selection costs roughly a third
of the training budget; ontological selection spends no GPU at all.}
\label{fig:cost}
\end{figure}

We did not obtain a measured wall-clock figure for the D4 arm: it failed
repeatedly with native crashes on the GPU tier available to us, and we report
the analytic estimate rather than a timing from different hardware, which
\S\ref{sec:metrics} rules out. The comparison above is therefore in FLOPs, not
seconds.

\subsection{Implications}

For practitioners: we found no evidence that ontological coverage selection
is worth implementing at this scale and budget. Its advantage over random
selection did not reach significance on the stratum it targets, and the
aggregate edge it does show is reproduced in full by permuted tags. If a cheap
selector is wanted, the operative variable appears to be example length at a
fixed step budget, which requires no ontology and no tagger.

For the ontology-guided adaptation literature: the flat-versus-propagated
ablation we proposed in \S\ref{sec:abl} now looks like the wrong question.
The prior question is whether the concept signal contributes anything over a
permuted control, and it should be asked first.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

\paragraph{Coverage is a proxy.} A passing mention counts equally with
substantive treatment. The method assumes mention approximates learning value;
failure of this assumption is the most likely source of a null result.

\paragraph{Rarity may anti-correlate with representativeness.} Coverage
selection favours examples mentioning rare concepts, which are often atypical.
The resulting subset may be less representative of the evaluation distribution
than a uniform sample, providing a mechanism by which the method could
\emph{underperform} uniform sampling rather than merely tie it.

\paragraph{Signal poverty relative to embeddings.} Ontological tags capture
only what the ontology encodes and the matcher detects, missing stylistic,
structural, and relational redundancy that embedding proximity captures. We
expect this to cost quality and aim to measure how much.

\paragraph{The ontology is an unpriced input.} Curated ontologies embody
substantial expert labour our accounting does not charge. The claim is scoped
to domains where such resources already exist---the same assumption made by
prior ontology-guided work.

\paragraph{Absolute savings are modest at our scale.} At a 200{,}000-example
pool, embedding-based selection costs GPU-minutes, not GPU-hours. The argument
is a ratio that scales linearly with pool size while training cost scales with
budget; the absolute figure at our scale is small by construction.

\paragraph{Statistical power.} The evaluation set contains 940 items, giving
roughly 255 items per concept-frequency stratum and a standard error near
three percentage points on stratified accuracy. Differences below that
threshold are undetectable by construction, and we report them as null rather
than as small effects. Our seed count is at the pre-registered minimum, and
the budget sweep and ablations specified in \S\ref{sec:abl} were not executed;
this is a single-budget, single-scale (1.5B), single-domain result.

\paragraph{Instruction data is already curated.} Unlike raw pretraining
corpora, instruction collections are typically deduplicated before release,
leaving less redundancy for any selection method to exploit. This works against
all arms equally but lowers the ceiling on achievable gains.

\section{Conclusion}

We proposed ontological concept coverage as a zero-GPU selection signal for
instruction tuning, positioned as a cheaper route to a comparable subset
rather than a better one, and pre-registered a placebo arm to test whether
the signal was doing the work. It was not. Coverage selection did not
separate from uniform sampling on the stratum it targets, and it did not
separate from the same procedure run over deliberately permuted tags. What
modest edge the selection arms hold belongs to the geometry of per-token
coverage selection---short, tag-dense examples---rather than to the ontology. Because the design fixes
the budget below the measured saturation point and reports tagging recall, we
can attribute the null to the mention-versus-learning-value gap rather than
to operating above $k^{\ast}$ or to a tagger that missed the corpus.

The negative result is the contribution we can defend. A free selector does
beat random selection at aggressive keep ratios; an ontology is not what makes
it work. Work claiming ontology-guided selection gains should run the permuted
control before attributing them to the ontology.

